{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovTraceMultiplicative","lean:module:QOU.Mass.ArchimedeanTraceBridge","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_assemble","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_assemble","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L162-L181","doc":"**So the free energy of an `m + 1`-component assembly is short by `m · log δ`** — the deficit is\nextensive in the number of components, not a fixed offset. For `⁴He` (`m = 3`) that is\n`3 · log [2]_q = 2.0957424747` at the proton-pinned `q₀`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_assemble","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_assemble","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L151-L160","doc":"**The `A`-component deficit is `δ^{A−1}`.** Assembling `m + 1` components multiplies the\nnormaliser by `δ^m` relative to the product of the parts, so the composite normalised trace is\nshort by exactly that factor. The two-fold `closureNorm_juxtapose` is `m = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_prod","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L143-L149","doc":"The closure-normalised traces of a family multiply into the normalisation at the **summed**\nexponent — the bookkeeping step, with no deficit yet. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.loopValue_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.loopValue_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L119-L127","doc":"**The substrate never sits at the exceptional point.** For `q > 1` the loop value exceeds one —\n`q + q⁻¹ > 1` — so `freeEnergy_closureNorm_additive_iff`'s escape hatch is closed and the `log δ`\nterm is always present. At `q₀` it is `log 2.01089686671 = 0.6985808249` per juxtaposition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.loopValue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.loopValue","kind":"def","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L116-L117","doc":"The canonical (Wenzl) loop value `δ = [2]_q = q + q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_additive_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_additive_iff","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L99-L114","doc":"**Additivity holds exactly when the loop value is trivial.** So `freeEnergy_juxtapose`'s reading\nis correct precisely in the case the substrate excludes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_juxtapose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.freeEnergy_closureNorm_juxtapose","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L79-L97","doc":"**So the free energy is additive only up to `log δ`.** Over the reals with positive amplitudes\nand a positive loop value, the composite closure-normalised free energy is the sum of the parts\n**minus** `log δ` — not the sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_juxtapose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm_juxtapose","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L69-L77","doc":"**Juxtaposition costs one loop factor.** Normalised traces do not simply multiply: combining a\n`(a+1)`-strand and a `(b+1)`-strand closure into an `(a+b+2)`-strand one loses one power of `δ`\nrelative to the product. This is the precise sense in which the unnormalised identity\n`tr(A ⊗ₖ B) = tr A · tr B` over-counts. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ClosureNormalisationDefect.closureNorm","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ClosureNormalisationDefect.lean#L64-L67","doc":"The closure-normalised trace of a braid on `k + 1` strands whose unnormalised trace is `t`:\ndivide by the loop value to the number of strands minus one. Indexed by `k = n − 1` so the exponent\nis a natural number. ","signatureChunk":0}]}